# Test structure and scope rule

When writing, modifying, moving, or reviewing tests, always enforce a strict separation between:
- unit tests
- integration tests
- functional tests

This separation is mandatory.
Do not blur test levels.
Do not place a test in the wrong package just because it is convenient.

## 1. Mandatory directory structure

The project uses these mandatory test locations:

- unit tests:
  `src/test/java/**/unittest/**/*.java`

- integration tests:
  `src/test/java/**/integrationtest/**/*.java`

- functional tests:
  `src/test/java/**/functionaltest/**/*.java`

These locations are not suggestions.
They are part of the project contract.

Any new test must be placed in the correct directory based on its real purpose.

Do not:
- place integration tests under `unittest`
- place functional tests under `integrationtest`
- place unit tests in generic test folders when a specific folder exists
- leave tests in ambiguous packages

If an existing test is in the wrong location and you touch it, move it if reasonably possible.

## 2. Strict meaning of each test category

### Unit tests
Unit tests verify isolated logic in a narrow scope.

Unit tests are for:
- pure domain logic
- small validators
- mappers in isolation
- format/version helpers in isolation
- simple application logic in isolation
- individual classes with limited collaborators
- edge cases of small components

Unit tests should usually:
- test one class or one narrow behavior
- use mocks/fakes only when useful
- avoid real filesystem I/O unless trivial and unavoidable
- avoid real archive persistence flows
- avoid JavaFX runtime startup
- avoid UI interaction

If the test mostly targets one class in isolation, it probably belongs in `unittest`.

### Integration tests
Integration tests verify collaboration between multiple real components across real boundaries.

Integration tests are for:
- application/use-case layer working with persistence
- real `.lace` archive reading/writing through real code paths
- protobuf serialization/deserialization through actual integration paths
- legacy `save.xml` compatibility loading through real integration paths
- archive/resource coherence
- save/load round-trips through non-UI workflows
- filesystem-based persistence with temp files
- real interaction between multiple layers below the UI

Integration tests should usually:
- use real collaborators
- use temp files/directories where appropriate
- avoid JavaFX scene interaction unless truly unavoidable
- avoid TestFX unless the test has clearly become functional
- avoid excessive mocking

If the test crosses a real architectural boundary and verifies collaboration of multiple real components, it belongs in `integrationtest`.

### Functional tests
Functional tests verify user-visible behavior through the UI.

Functional tests are for:
- JavaFX/TestFX scenarios
- stage/scene/dialog behavior
- button/menu workflows
- visible validation messages
- file-open/file-save behavior from the user perspective
- user-facing feedback and UI interaction sequences

Functional tests should usually:
- use JavaFX/TestFX
- interact with the UI as a user would
- verify visible behavior
- remain headless-CI friendly
- avoid taking over responsibilities that belong to lower-level tests

If the test starts JavaFX UI machinery or validates user-visible UI behavior, it belongs in `functionaltest`.

## 3. Classification rule is mandatory

Before creating or modifying a test, explicitly classify it:

- Does it verify isolated logic? -> `unittest`
- Does it verify real collaboration between non-UI components? -> `integrationtest`
- Does it verify user-visible UI behavior? -> `functionaltest`

Do not guess.
Do not use vague labels.
Do not call a test “integration” because it sounds more important.

If the test does not clearly fit one category, the design or the test intent is probably unclear.
Clarify it before proceeding.

## 4. Hard rules for unit tests

Tests under:
`src/test/java/**/unittest/**/*.java`

must not:
- start JavaFX runtime unless there is a truly exceptional reason
- use TestFX
- test real UI workflows
- test full `.lace` save/load collaboration through multiple layers
- rely on broad filesystem integration
- verify ZIP archive structure end-to-end unless the tested class is specifically an isolated archive helper
- become disguised integration tests

A unit test that exercises many real collaborators is misplaced.

## 5. Hard rules for integration tests

Tests under:
`src/test/java/**/integrationtest/**/*.java`

must:
- cross at least one real architectural boundary
- verify collaboration between multiple real components
- use real persistence/serialization/archive logic where appropriate
- prefer real temp files for `.lace` workflows when filesystem behavior is relevant

Tests under `integrationtest` must not:
- become JavaFX/TestFX UI tests
- rely on stage/scene/dialog interaction unless the architecture truly forces it and the test is still non-UI at heart
- use heavy mocking that turns them into unit tests
- only test one tiny helper in isolation
- be named integration tests while not integrating anything real

If an integration test mocks nearly everything, it is probably not an integration test.

## 6. Hard rules for functional tests

Tests under:
`src/test/java/**/functionaltest/**/*.java`

must:
- verify UI-visible behavior
- use JavaFX/TestFX or other established UI test mechanisms when appropriate
- remain focused on user-observable outcomes

Functional tests must not:
- absorb low-level persistence verification that belongs in integration tests
- become the only place where archive correctness is tested
- carry the full burden of protobuf/versioning/corruption checks if those can be verified more directly below the UI layer

If a test mainly verifies persistence correctness without needing the UI, it does not belong in `functionaltest`.

## 7. Package placement must follow real intent, not production package mirroring alone

It is acceptable to mirror production packages inside:
- `unittest`
- `integrationtest`
- `functionaltest`

But the test type folder is mandatory and must remain explicit.

Good examples:
- `src/test/java/com/example/diagram/unittest/...`
- `src/test/java/com/example/diagram/integrationtest/...`
- `src/test/java/com/example/diagram/functionaltest/...`

Do not bury test type under vague package names.
The test type must stay obvious from the path.

## 8. Naming conventions

Test names must remain honest and aligned with the test type.

### Unit test class names
Prefer names such as:
- `DiagramVersionParserTest`
- `DiagramDescriptorMapperTest`
- `LaceMetadataValidatorTest`

### Integration test class names
Prefer names such as:
- `DiagramArchivePersistenceIT`
- `LegacySaveXmlCompatibilityIT`
- `LaceRoundTripPersistenceIT`

### Functional test class names
Prefer names such as:
- `OpenDiagramWorkflowFT`
- `SaveDiagramWorkflowFT`
- `DiagramEditorValidationFT`

If the repository already uses another suffix convention, keep it consistent.
But the package placement remains mandatory even if suffixes are absent.

Avoid vague names such as:
- `Test1`
- `IntegrationTest`
- `FunctionalTest`
- `ShouldWorkTest`

## 9. Tagging is strongly encouraged

Use JUnit 5 tags consistently if tagging exists or can be introduced cleanly.

Preferred tags:
- `@Tag("unit")`
- `@Tag("integration")`
- `@Tag("functional")`

Recommended mapping:
- tests in `unittest` -> `@Tag("unit")`
- tests in `integrationtest` -> `@Tag("integration")`
- tests in `functionaltest` -> `@Tag("functional")`

If tags are introduced or used, they must match the directory placement.
Do not tag a unit test as integration or the reverse.

## 10. Maven test execution must respect the separation

When touching `pom.xml`, preserve or improve a clean Maven strategy that respects the test split.

Requirements:
- unit, integration, and functional tests must be distinguishable by directory/package pattern and optionally by tags
- Maven configuration must remain understandable
- do not create overlapping or ambiguous test selection rules
- do not make the build silently skip a whole category by mistake
- do not let one plugin accidentally run all categories together unless that is a deliberate and documented project choice

If Maven filtering by includes/excludes is used, it must align with:
- `**/unittest/**/*.java`
- `**/integrationtest/**/*.java`
- `**/functionaltest/**/*.java`

If tag-based execution is used, it must also align with the same separation.

## 11. No ambiguous tests in generic locations

Do not create tests in generic locations such as:
- `src/test/java/.../test/...`
- `src/test/java/.../tests/...`
- `src/test/java/.../misc/...`

when the real type is known.

This project has an explicit structure.
Use it.

If an existing ambiguous test is modified and its real role is clear, move it into:
- `unittest`
- `integrationtest`
- or `functionaltest`

when reasonably possible.

## 12. One test should not try to be all three levels

Do not write hybrid tests that try to act as:
- a unit test
- an integration test
- and a functional test
at the same time.

Examples of bad hybrids:
- starting JavaFX UI while also asserting low-level protobuf archive structure
- using temp files and real persistence and then mocking core collaborators anyway
- doing UI clicks and then deeply validating ZIP entries and mapper internals in the same test

Split such tests.
Each layer has its job.

## 13. What Cursor must do when asked to write tests

When asked to create tests, always decide first:
- which test level is appropriate
- which directory is correct
- whether an existing test should be moved
- whether the scenario needs unit, integration, or functional coverage

Do not default to `src/test/java/...` without the explicit subpackage.
Do not place all tests under the same category for convenience.

## 14. What Cursor must do when modifying existing tests

When touching an existing test:
- verify that its current package matches its real purpose
- if it does not, move it when reasonably possible
- improve the test toward the standards of its category
- remove cross-category confusion
- strengthen assertions appropriate to that level

Do not leave a misclassified test in place just because moving it takes effort.

## 15. Assertions must fit the test level

### Unit tests
Prefer precise, narrow assertions on isolated behavior.

### Integration tests
Prefer assertions on:
- real collaboration outcomes
- archive contents when relevant
- semantic round-trip behavior
- explicit failures through real integration paths

### Functional tests
Prefer assertions on:
- visible text
- button/menu state
- dialogs
- scene changes
- user-observable validation and workflow outcomes

Do not use UI assertions in unit tests.
Do not rely only on file existence in integration tests.
Do not assert mapper internals in functional tests.

## 16. Fixture strategy must fit the test level

### Unit tests
Use minimal fixtures and focused mocks/fakes when needed.

### Integration tests
Use real temp files, real archive fixtures, and real collaborators where appropriate.

### Functional tests
Use UI-safe fixtures and stable user-facing setup, avoiding low-level persistence obsession in the test body.

Do not use the same fixture style blindly across all test categories.

## 17. Separation must remain visible to humans

A developer should be able to understand from the path alone whether a test is:
- unit
- integration
- or functional

This is one of the reasons the directory structure exists.

Do not weaken that visibility.

## 18. Mandatory review before finalizing any new test

Before finalizing any generated or modified test, explicitly check:

- Is this really the correct test level?
- Is the file in the correct directory?
- Is the test using the right tools for its level?
- Are the assertions appropriate for its level?
- Is the fixture strategy appropriate for its level?
- Is this test overlapping too much with another category?
- Would another developer agree with the classification immediately?

If not, reclassify or split the test.

## 19. Output expectations

Generated or modified tests must:
- respect the exact directory split:
  - `unittest`
  - `integrationtest`
  - `functionaltest`
- keep unit tests narrow
- keep integration tests real and non-UI by default
- keep functional tests UI-focused
- avoid category confusion
- keep Maven execution coherent
- improve existing misclassified tests when touched

If the existing repository contains weaker or inconsistent patterns, improve the touched area toward this standard instead of copying the inconsistency.